Here is a complete, compilable example of how to generate a Table of Contents (ToC) in LaTeX.

The specific command responsible for generating the list is **`\tableofcontents`**. LaTeX automatically pulls the headings from your `\section`, `\subsection`, and `\subsubsection` commands to populate it.

### LaTeX Code Example

Save the following code in a file named `example.tex`:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\begin{document}

% 1. This command generates the Table of Contents automatically
\tableofcontents

% This command moves the rest of the content to a new page
% (Optional, but makes the document look cleaner)
\newpage 

% 2. The content sections below will populate the Table of Contents
\section{Introduction}
This is the first section. LaTeX will add "1 Introduction" to the ToC.

\section{Literature Review}
This is the second section.

    \subsection{History of the Topic}
    This is a subsection. It will appear indented in the ToC as "2.1".

    \subsection{Current Trends}
    This is another subsection.

\section{Methodology}
This is the third section.

    \subsection{Data Collection}

        \subsubsection{Surveys}
        This is a sub-subsection. Depending on settings, this may or may not appear in the ToC.

        \subsubsection{Interviews}
        Another sub-subsection.

\section{Conclusion}
The final section.

\end{document}
```

### Important: How to Compile
To see the Table of Contents appear, you usually need to **compile the document twice**.

1.  **First Compile:** LaTeX goes through the document and writes the section titles and page numbers into a temporary file (ending in `.toc`). At this stage, the ToC in your PDF might appear empty or show `??`.
2.  **Second Compile:** LaTeX reads the `.toc` file created in the first step and prints the actual table in your document.

Here is the short answer: **Always use `.equals()` to compare the content of Strings in Java.**

The `==` operator is not "bad," but it checks for something completely different than what you usually want when comparing text.

Here is the breakdown of the difference, why your bug happened, and when to use which.

### 1. The Difference: Reference vs. Value

*   **`==` checks Reference Equality (Memory Address):**
    It checks if the two variables point to the **exact same object** in the computer's memory. It asks: *"Are these two variables actually the same instance?"*
*   **`.equals()` checks Value Equality (Content):**
    It checks if the **characters** inside the objects are identical. It asks: *"Do these two strings contain the same text?"*

### 2. Why `==` worked sometimes (The String Pool)

You might wonder why `==` worked for a while. This is due to a Java optimization called the **String Constant Pool**.

If you create strings using literals (without `new`), Java saves memory by re-using the same object.

```java
String a = "Hello";
String b = "Hello";

// This returns TRUE because Java made 'a' and 'b' point 
// to the exact same object in the pool to save memory.
System.out.println(a == b); 
```

### 3. Why `==` failed (The Bug)

As soon as a String is created dynamically (via user input, concatenation, or the `new` keyword), Java creates a **new** memory object, even if the text is the same.

```java
String a = "Hello";
String b = new String("Hello"); // Forces a new memory slot

// This returns FALSE. They have different memory addresses.
System.out.println(a == b); 

// This returns TRUE. They contain the same text.
System.out.println(a.equals(b)); 
```

### Summary: When to use what?

| Operator | Use Case | Example |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`.equals()`** | **99% of the time.** Use this when you want to know if two strings contain the same text. | `if (input.equals("exit"))` |
| **`==`** | **Rarely.** Use this only if you specifically need to know if two variables refer to the exact same object in memory, or when checking for `null`. | `if (str == null)` |

### Bonus Tip: Null Safety

One common issue with `.equals()` is that if the first string is `null`, your program will crash with a `NullPointerException`.

**Bad:**
```java
String input = null;
if (input.equals("test")) { // CRASH!
    // ...
}
```

**Good (Yoda Notation):**
Put the known string literal first.
```java
String input = null;
if ("test".equals(input)) { // Returns false, no crash
    // ...
}
```

**Best (Java 7+):**
Use the Objects utility class.
```java
import java.util.Objects;

if (Objects.equals(input, "test")) { // Handles nulls safely
    // ...
}
```

